{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Unit Conversion Factor.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Unit Conversion Factor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"unit conversion factor unit conversion factor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Unit Conversion Factor.md","fileName":"Unit Conversion Factor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Unit Conversion Factor","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Unit Algebra and Measurement Precision]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Unit Algebra and Measurement Precision","Reciprocal Conversion Factors","Physical Quantity as Value and Unit","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Unit Conversion Factor.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Unit Conversion Factor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"unit conversion factor unit conversion factor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Unit Conversion Factor.md","fileName":"Unit Conversion Factor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Unit Conversion Factor","heading":"Unit Conversion Factor","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A conversion factor is a ratio between compatible unit scales. Written with both units, it can be multiplied by a measured value so the starting unit cancels and the desired unit remains. For example, the book uses $0.3048\\ \\mathrm{m/ft}$ to convert a feet-per-second value into meters per second: the feet cancel, and the seconds are unchanged. The orientation of the ratio matters even when its number is familiar. See [[Reciprocal Conversion Factors]] and [[Physical Quantity as Value and Unit]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Unit Algebra and Measurement Precision","Reciprocal Conversion Factors","Physical Quantity as Value and Unit","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"]